 10 | /**
 11 |  * DOC: L3VPN
 12 |  *
 13 |  * The L3VPN protocol implements RFC 4364 BGP/MPLS VPNs using MPLS backbone.
 14 |  * It works similarly to pipe. It connects IP table (one per VRF) with (global)
 15 |  * VPN table. Routes passed from VPN table to IP table are stripped of RD and
 16 |  * filtered by import targets, routes passed in the other direction are extended
 17 |  * with RD, MPLS labels and export targets in extended communities. Separate
 18 |  * MPLS channel is used to announce MPLS routes for the labels.
 19 |  *
 20 |  * Note that in contrast to the pipe protocol, L3VPN protocol has both IPv4 and
 21 |  * IPv6 channels in one instance, Also both IP and VPN channels are presented to
 22 |  * users as separate channels, although that will change in the future.
 23 |  *
 24 |  * The L3VPN protocol has different default preferences on IP and VPN sides.
 25 |  * The reason is that in import direction (VPN->IP) routes should have lower
 26 |  * preferences that ones received from local CE (perhaps by EBGP), while in
 27 |  * export direction (IP->VPN) routes should have higher preferences that ones
 28 |  * received from remote PEs (by IBGP).
 29 |  *
 30 |  * Supported standards:
 31 |  * RFC 4364 - BGP/MPLS IP Virtual Private Networks (L3VPN)
 32 |  */
 33 | 
