 346 | /**
 347 |  * DOC: Sockets
 348 |  *
 349 |  * Socket resources represent network connections. Their data structure (&socket)
 350 |  * contains a lot of fields defining the exact type of the socket, the local and
 351 |  * remote addresses and ports, pointers to socket buffers and finally pointers to
 352 |  * hook functions to be called when new data have arrived to the receive buffer
 353 |  * (@rx_hook), when the contents of the transmit buffer have been transmitted
 354 |  * (@tx_hook) and when an error or connection close occurs (@err_hook).
 355 |  *
 356 |  * Freeing of sockets from inside socket hooks is perfectly safe.
 357 |  */
